course: french
lesson: 38
title: Olympiad: gender-pattern puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A French gender puzzle is a detective story: the noun hides its gender, and you must find it from clues - the ending of the word, or the little words and endings around it.
explain: Endings are the first clue: -ment, -age and -eau usually mean masculine, while -tion, -ette, -ure and -té usually mean feminine - but a good detective also remembers the famous rebels, like la plage and l'été.
explain: The second clue is the company a noun keeps: mon or ma, il or elle, un or une, and an extra e on a describing word all give the gender away, even when l' hides it.
explain: Picture finding a note on the kitchen table: "Je suis fatiguée mais contente !" - nobody signed it, but the two silent e's tell you it was written by a girl or a woman.
explain: Some words even change meaning with their gender, so the le or la is the only clue to what the sentence means.
fact: Using endings: le bâtiment (-ment), une émotion (-tion), le fromage (-age), une chaussette (-ette), le château (-eau), une aventure (-ure).
fact: Most -age words are masculine, but la plage, la page, la cage and l'image are feminine exceptions.
fact: La poste is the post office; le poste or un poste is a job: "Ma sœur a un nouveau poste" means my sister has a new job.
fact: When l' hides the gender, an adjective can show it: l'école est grande (feminine), l'arbre est grand and l'hôtel est petit (masculine).
fact: Liberté, égalité, fraternité are all feminine, like most -té words; l'été (summer) and le côté (the side) are masculine exceptions.
fact: Job words change in patterns: boulanger to boulangère, chanteur to chanteuse, acteur to actrice; so danseur gives danseuse, fermier gives fermière and directeur gives directrice.
fact: Countries: en Italie and en Allemagne (feminine), au Brésil and au Mexique (masculine); le Mexique ends in -e but is masculine.
fact: "Mon doudou ? Il est sous le lit." shows that doudou is masculine twice: mon, not ma, and il, not elle.
fact: In la lune, la mer, la table, le soleil, la plage, only le soleil is masculine; in German the sun is feminine and the moon masculine, which trips people up.
fact: "Je suis fatiguée mais contente !" was written by a girl or woman; a boy would write fatigué and content.
fact: Camille and Sacha can be names for boys or girls in French: "mon ami Camille" is a boy, "mon amie Sacha est sportive" is a girl.
fact: Un tour is a trip or a turn, but une tour is a tower, so the Eiffel Tower is la tour Eiffel.
exercise: Which three of these are feminine: bâtiment, émotion, fromage, chaussette, château, aventure? | émotion, chaussette, aventure | bâtiment, fromage, château | émotion, fromage, aventure | chaussette, château, bâtiment
exercise: Find the rebel: le voyage, le garage, ? plage, le fromage. | la plage | le plage | les plage | l'plage
exercise: "Ma sœur a un nouveau poste." What does she have? | A new job | A new post office | A new letter | A new stamp
exercise: "Mon frère travaille à la poste." Where does he work? | At the post office | At a new job | At the airport | On a fence post
exercise: L'arbre est grand. L'école est grande. L'hôtel est petit. Which word is feminine? | école | arbre | hôtel | all three
exercise: What gender are liberté, égalité and fraternité? | All feminine | All masculine | Two feminine, one masculine | It depends on the town
exercise: Which -té word breaks the rule and is masculine? | l'été, summer | la santé, health | la beauté, beauty | la société, society
exercise: Un danseur becomes... | une danseuse | une danseure | une danstrice | une danseurette
exercise: Un fermier becomes... | une fermière | une fermieuse | une fermiere | une fermiertrice
exercise: Un directeur becomes... | une directrice | une directeuse | une directeure | une directière
exercise: Fill in: ___ Italie, ___ Brésil, ___ Allemagne, ___ Mexique. | en, au, en, au | en, au, en, en | au, en, au, en | en, en, en, au
exercise: Which country ends in -e but is masculine? | le Mexique | la France | l'Italie | l'Allemagne
exercise: "Mon doudou ? Il est sous le lit." Is doudou le or la? | le, from mon and il | la, because it's soft | la, from sous and lit | le, because it's under the bed
exercise: Odd one out: la lune, la mer, la table, le soleil, la plage. | le soleil, the only masculine word | la mer, the only wet thing | la plage, the only -age word | la lune, the only thing in the sky
exercise: A note says "Je suis fatiguée mais contente !" Who wrote it? | A girl or a woman | A boy or a man | Two boys together | Nobody can tell
exercise: "Mon ami Camille est drôle." Is this Camille a boy or a girl? | A boy, because ami has no e | A girl, because Camille is a girl's name | A girl, because drôle ends in e | Nobody can tell from the sentence
test: ★★★ Harry from Leeds goes, "Fromage is soft and creamy, so it's a girl word, like." Use the ending to put him right. | It ends in -age, so it's le fromage | He's right, it's la fromage | It ends in -age, so it's la fromage | Cheese has no gender in French
test: ★★★ Ruby from Belfast says, "Sure they all end in -age, it's le plage." Her teacher smiles. Why? | Plage is a famous feminine exception | Ruby is right, it's le plage | Plage is plural, so it's les plage | Plage starts with a vowel, so it's l'plage
test: ★★★ Josh from Sydney reads "Mon frère travaille à la poste. Ma sœur a un nouveau poste." He goes, "Yeah nah, same word twice?" What is the true meaning? | Brother at the post office; sister has a new job | Both work at the post office | Both have new jobs | Brother has a job; sister has a post office
test: ★★★ Amira from Leicester says, "The l' is hiding it, innit." In L'arbre est grand. L'école est grande. L'hôtel est petit., how many words are feminine? | One | Two | Three | None
test: ★★★ Theo from Bristol says "la été" because "-té words are girls, proper job". Fix it. | l'été, and été is masculine | la été is right | le été is right | les été is right
test: ★★★ Isla from Inverness writes "une directeure" for a woman head teacher. Which example should she copy? | acteur to actrice, so directrice | chanteur to chanteuse, so directeuse | boulanger to boulangère, so directère | Just add e: directeure is right
test: ★★★ Leo from Wellington goes, "Chur, Mexique ends in e, so en Mexique." What's the catch? | Le Mexique is masculine, so au Mexique | He's right, en Mexique | It should be à Mexique | It should be aux Mexique
test: ★★★ Olivia from Ohio says doudou must be "la" because "it's soft and cute, no cap". Which clues prove her wrong? | mon and il, both masculine | sous and lit, both masculine | le lit, which is masculine | Nothing, she's right
test: ★★★ Charlie from Kent says, "The sun's the boy, then. Easy." Why might a German speaker get it wrong? | In German the sun is feminine and the moon masculine | In German there are no genders | German has the same genders as French | In German the table is masculine
test: ★★★ Hannah from Edinburgh reads "Je suis fatigué mais content !" (no extra e's) on a note. Who wrote it, her brother or her sister? | Her brother | Her sister | Either, you can't tell | Both of them together
test: ★★★ Ava from Ohio reads "Mon amie Sacha est sportive" and says, "Sacha's a boy's name, so the sentence is wrong." What's true? | Here Sacha is a girl: amie and sportive show it | She's right, it should be mon ami Sacha est sportif | Sacha can never be a girl's name in French | The sentence is about two different people
test: ★★★ What would happen if you said "le tour Eiffel" to a Parisian? | You'd be talking about a trip, not the tower | It would be perfectly correct | You'd be talking about a cake | Nobody would understand anything
exam: Ascertain the gender of the noun "chaussette" on the basis of its terminal syllable. | Feminine, the ending -ette being characteristic thereof | Masculine, the ending -ette being characteristic thereof | Indeterminable without a dictionary | Feminine, by reason of its meaning
exam: Identify the noun ending in -té which constitutes an exception to the general feminine gender of such nouns. | été | liberté | santé | beauté
exam: Select the feminine form corresponding to the masculine noun "directeur". | directrice | directeuse | directeure | directière
exam: In the statement "Mon doudou ? Il est sous le lit.", determine the gender of the noun doudou. | Masculine | Feminine | Both, interchangeably | Neither
